//? Title: Robust summaries and sample quantiles
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

A single large observation can pull the arithmetic mean and inflate the
sample standard deviation. The trimmed mean removes an equal number of
observations from each end. The Winsorized summaries replace those
observations with the nearest retained endpoints. The median absolute
deviation measures spread around the sample median.

> Summarize a sample with one large observation

```{cpp, id=robust-univariate, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/robust_summary.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> sample{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 100};
    const auto summary = trimmed_summary(sample, .2);
    std::println("| Summary | Value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: |");
    std::println("| Arithmetic mean | {:.2f} |", std::accumulate(sample.begin(), sample.end(), 0.0) / sample.size());
    std::println("| Trimmed mean | {:.2f} |", summary.trimmed_mean);
    std::println("| Winsorized variance | {:.2f} |", summary.winsorized_variance);
    std::println("| Raw MAD | {:.2f} |", median_absolute_deviation(sample, {}, 1));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Summary | Value |
| --- | ---: |
| Arithmetic mean | 18.33 |
| Trimmed mean | 2.50 |
| Winsorized variance | 1.90 |
| Raw MAD | 1.50 |
// result_end

Trimming 20% of six observations removes one value per tail. Winsorizing
changes the sample to `1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4`. Its squared deviations about 2.5
sum to 9.5, giving a sample variance of 1.9. The raw MAD is 1.5, the median
of the six absolute deviations about 2.5.

> Compare quartile conventions

An empirical quantile selects a value from the ordered sample. A convention
that interpolates can give a value between observations. With survey-style
weights, the inverse empirical CDF uses cumulative mass in place of rank.

```{cpp, id=sample-quantiles, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/robust_summary.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> values{0, 10, 20, 40};
    const std::vector<double> probs{.25, .5, .75};
    std::println("| Method | First quartile | Median | Third quartile |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto print_row = [](const char* method, const std::vector<double>& q) {
        std::println("| {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} |", method, q[0], q[1], q[2]);
    };
    print_row("Type 1 (empirical)", sample_quantiles(values, probs, 1));
    print_row("Type 7 (linear interpolation)", sample_quantiles(values, probs));
    print_row("Weighted empirical", weighted_empirical_quantiles(values, {1, 1, 4, 2}, probs));
    print_row("Harrell–Davis", harrell_davis_quantiles(values, probs));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Method | First quartile | Median | Third quartile |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Type 1 (empirical) | 0.00 | 10.00 | 20.00 |
| Type 7 (linear interpolation) | 7.50 | 15.00 | 25.00 |
| Weighted empirical | 10.00 | 20.00 | 20.00 |
| Harrell–Davis | 5.51 | 16.27 | 30.27 |
// result_end

The value 20 carries half the weighted mass. The cumulative weight first
reaches both 50% and 75% at that value.
Harrell–Davis uses contributions from every order statistic. Its weights
come from a beta distribution centered at the requested probability.

> Put an interval on the population median

For independent draws from a continuous distribution, the count below the
population median follows a binomial distribution with probability one-half.
This gives an interval whose endpoints are sample order statistics. Its
coverage can exceed the requested level because the endpoint ranks are integers.

```{cpp, id=exact-quantile-interval, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/robust_summary.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    const auto interval = datamunge::stats::exact_quantile_interval({0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 100}, .5);
    std::println("Median interval [{:.0f}, {:.0f}] uses ranks {} and {} and has {:.3f}% coverage.",
                 interval.lower, interval.upper, interval.lower_rank, interval.upper_rank,
                 100 * interval.coverage);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Median interval [0, 100] uses ranks 1 and 6 and has 96.875% coverage.
// result_end

> Enclose the whole CDF

The Dvoretzky–Kiefer–Wolfowitz band has a single half-width determined by the
sample size and desired simultaneous coverage. Values at a tied observation
include every observation equal to it.

```{cpp, id=empirical-cdf-band, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/robust_summary.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    const datamunge::stats::EmpiricalCdfBand band({7, 1, 1, 3, 4, 3, 4, 5}, .8);
    std::println("| x | Empirical CDF | Lower | Upper |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto& point : band.knots())
        std::println("| {:.0f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                     point.x, point.cdf, point.lower, point.upper);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| x | Empirical CDF | Lower | Upper |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.250 | 0.000 | 0.629 |
| 3 | 0.500 | 0.121 | 0.879 |
| 4 | 0.750 | 0.371 | 1.000 |
| 5 | 0.875 | 0.496 | 1.000 |
| 7 | 1.000 | 0.621 | 1.000 |
// result_end

> Compare robust measures of spread

Qn selects a low quantile of the distances between distinct observations.
Sn takes a median of median distances. Huber's estimate clips deviations
at a multiple of its current scale and updates both scale and location.
Here five observations range from −2 to 2, and the sixth is 100.

```{cpp, id=robust-scale, cache=yes, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/robust_summary.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<double> sample{-2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 100};
    const auto huber = huber_location_scale(sample);
    std::println("| Measure | Value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: |");
    std::println("| Qn scale | {:.3f} |", qn_scale(sample));
    std::println("| Sn scale | {:.3f} |", sn_scale(sample));
    std::println("| Huber scale | {:.3f} |", huber.scale);
    std::println("\nHuber location is {:.3f}. The fit took {} updates. Converged = {}.",
                 huber.location, huber.iterations, huber.converged);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Measure | Value |
| --- | ---: |
| Qn scale | 2.717 |
| Sn scale | 2.369 |
| Huber scale | 2.896 |

Huber location is 0.869. The fit took 54 updates. Converged = true.
// result_end
